  "title": "Kourovka problem 21.86 (main)",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "(M. Gromov, B. Weiss). A group G is said to be sofic if for every finite set\nF ⊆ G containing 1 and every ε > 0 there exist n ∈ N and a map φ : F → Sn such\nthat φ(1) = 1, d(φ(gh), φ(g)φ(h)) < ε for all g, h such that gh ∈ F , φ(g) does not\nhave fixed points for every g ∈ F \\ {1}.\n   Is every group sofic?                                               A. Lubotzky",
